- Story & Context: In the sub-freezing winter of 1994, Oldřich Vaneček sat shivering in his Prague workshop when a thumb-sized chondrite bolide punched a hole through his skylight and lodged itself into the brass escapement gear of a 200-year-old French mantle clock. Rather than repairing the mechanism, Vaneček mic’d the sputtering, ticking assembly using a beat-up Shure SM57 and routed the bizarre 84-BPM mechanical oscillation into his Leslie speaker cabinet while Maeve blew dizzying bebop arpeggios through a mute packed with meteor gravel. The resulting tape was an accident of interstellar friction and cassette tape drag that defined the genre, immortalized when Oldřich leaned into the talkback mic and crooned, "the sky just jammed my pendulum, and the tape won't stop rolling."
