Track 3: The Microcassette Spits Meteor Dust
- Story & Context: Folklore claims this mythical final recording was discovered inside a waterlogged answering machine retrieved from the flooded cellars beneath the old Greenwich Observatory during the spring thaws of 1998. When archivist Nigel Finch scrubbed the iron-oxidized tape heads with rubbing alcohol, the microcassette miraculously began looping backwards at 88 BPM, playing a deep, swinging organ vamp weighted by extraterrestrial gravity that made every brass clock in the room hum in sympathetic harmony. The track preserved the final, transcendent jam of the scene before the original workshop was shuttered forever, punctuated by a dying, tape-flanged vocal that crackled through the speaker: "when the gears wind down into iron gravel, the stars keep swinging in four-four time."
